{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgXFE5HRMXvt9pVqtKmg1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Next I'll write the abstract and introduction."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1930,"cache_read_input_tokens":69700,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1930},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a7259184-e90b-461a-a0ed-e4b7ad9b7a2d","uuid":"6db93397-cee5-4ee3-852c-420ac980f63c","timestamp":"2026-10-29T21:34:17.048Z","request_id":"req_011CgXFE54XgBqC9sd487s5T"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgXFE5HRMXvt9pVqtKmg1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Wnre7a2tvs9cGwYkPuYDFz","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat > sections/abstract.tex <<'EOF'\nText reaches language models with formatting the user never sees: Windows line endings, no-break spaces copied from web pages, trailing spaces, and typographic quotes and dashes from word processors.\nWe ask whether such meaning-preserving edits change model outputs, and which kind matters more.\nWe apply ten invisible whitespace and punctuation edits to three classification tasks and two code-generation tasks.\nWe run 11 open models from 0.5B to 14.8B parameters with greedy decoding (239,616 generations), and we compare every edit against a byte-identical re-run of the same prompt.\nA single invisible edit flips 4.9\\% of classification labels (95\\% CI 4.6--5.1\\%), while the identical re-run flips 0.02\\%.\nContrary to our hypothesis, whitespace edits flip 7.1\\% of labels and punctuation edits only 2.2\\%. Whitespace is more disruptive for every model and on both task types, and it stays more disruptive when we match the number of prompt tokens the edit changes.\nModels under 3B parameters flip labels 2.2$\\times$ as often as larger ones (CI 2.0--2.4).\nFor code, 36\\% of generations change text under an edit, but only 5.1\\% change their unit-test outcome, about the same rate as classification label flips.\nNet accuracy moves by less than 1 percentage point per model, because flips go in both directions and concentrate on items the model already gets wrong.\nFormatting therefore adds variance rather than bias. Practitioners should normalise whitespace before inference and report results over several formattings.\nEOF\ncat > sections/introduction.tex <<'EOF'\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nTwo prompts can look the same to a person and still differ in bytes.\nA review pasted from a web page carries no-break spaces (U+00A0) instead of ordinary spaces.\nA file saved on Windows ends its lines with \\texttt{\\textbackslash r\\textbackslash n}.\nA word processor replaces straight quotes with curly ones and hyphens with dashes.\nNone of these edits changes the meaning of the text, and most are invisible on screen.\nOur main question is simple: {\\bf do these invisible edits change what a language model outputs?}\n\nThe answer matters for anyone who measures or deploys LLMs.\nIf invisible formatting changes outputs, then benchmark numbers contain a hidden formatting term, A/B tests of prompt variants are confounded, and the same input behaves differently depending on the editor or operating system that produced it.\nSmall on-device models are a particular concern, because practitioners often assume they are brittle but rarely know by how much.\n\nPrior work shows that LLMs are sensitive to prompt design.\nChanging separators, casing or field names in a few-shot template can move accuracy by tens of points~\\cite{sclar2024quantifying}, single-prompt evaluations give unstable rankings~\\cite{mizrahi2024state}, and sensitivity varies across models and tasks~\\cite{zhuo2024prosa}.\nHowever, these studies perturb \\emph{visible} template structure.\nCharacter-level robustness work studies typos and adversarial edits~\\cite{belinkov2018synthetic,pruthi2019combating,wang2021textflint}, which a reader can also see.\nWe lack a controlled comparison of edits that a reader cannot see, one that separates whitespace from punctuation, spans model sizes within families, covers both classification and code, and measures each effect against the noise floor of inference itself.\n\nWe fill this gap with a controlled study.\nWe define five whitespace edits (trailing spaces, CRLF line endings, double spaces, trailing newlines, and no-break spaces or tabs) and five punctuation edits (curly quotes, curly apostrophes, Unicode dashes, the ellipsis character, and all four combined).\nWe apply each edit to every item of \\sst, \\agnews, \\boolq, \\humaneval and \\mbpp, and we run 11 open instruction-tuned models from the \\qwentwo, \\qwenthree and \\llama families, ranging from 0.5B to 14.8B parameters.\nEvery perturbed output is compared with the same model's output on the canonical prompt.\nEvery item also gets a \\emph{control} condition: the byte-identical canonical prompt placed at another batch position, which measures how often outputs change for no reason at all.\nFinally, we pass every prompt through each model's chat template and tokenizer to check whether the edit survives and how many tokens it changes.\n\n\\Figref{fig:per_condition} summarizes the main result.\nA single invisible edit flips 4.9\\% of classification labels on average, more than 200 times the 0.02\\% control rate.\nWe expected punctuation to matter more than whitespace. The opposite holds: whitespace edits flip 7.1\\% of labels and punctuation edits 2.2\\%, and whitespace is worse for all 11 models.\nPart of the gap comes from token footprint, because a double-space or no-break-space edit re-tokenizes every word. Even at a matched footprint, though, whitespace is worse: a trailing \\texttt{\\textbackslash n\\textbackslash n} (about one changed token) flips 3.4\\% of labels, while a Unicode dash (1.3 tokens) flips 1.3\\%.\nModels under 3B parameters flip 2.2$\\times$ as often as larger models on classification, but code shows no size effect.\nCode text changes in 36\\% of generations, yet only 5.1\\% of programs change their test outcome.\n\nOur contributions are:\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,topsep=0pt]\n    \\item We propose a taxonomy of ten invisible formatting edits, split into whitespace and punctuation families, with code-safe variants that leave programs valid.\n    \\item We conduct a controlled study over 11 open models, 5 tasks and 239,616 greedy generations, with a byte-identical re-run as the noise floor and an exact replication in a separate engine.\n    \\item We show that whitespace edits are 2--5$\\times$ more disruptive than punctuation edits, that small models are about 2$\\times$ more label-sensitive, and that most code changes are stylistic rather than functional.\n    \\item We trace the effect to the token level. Edits that the chat template removes, such as trailing newlines under \\llama templates, have zero effect, and divergence grows with the number of changed tokens without fully explaining the whitespace--punctuation gap.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\Secref{sec:related} reviews related work, \\secref{sec:method} describes our setup, \\secref{sec:results} reports results, and \\secref{sec:discussion} discusses mechanisms, implications and limitations.\nEOF","description":"Write abstract and introduction"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1930,"cache_read_input_tokens":69700,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1930},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a7259184-e90b-461a-a0ed-e4b7ad9b7a2d","uuid":"67dbebba-1f1d-4217-b33d-5054e49a9f92","timestamp":"2026-10-29T21:34:38.152Z","request_id":"req_011CgXFE54XgBqC9sd487s5T","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Wnre7a2tvs9cGwYkPuYDFz":{"command":"cd /data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/invisible_formatting_effects_20261029_120001_qr78st90/paper_draft && cat > sections/abstract.tex <<'EOF'\nText reaches language models with formatting the user never sees: Windows line endings, no-break spaces copied from web pages, trailing spaces, and typographic quotes and dashes from word processors.\nWe ask whether such meaning-preserving edits change model outputs, and which kind matters more.\nWe apply ten invisible whitespace and punctuation edits to three classification tasks and two code-generation tasks.\nWe run 11 open models from 0.5B to 14.8B parameters with greedy decoding (239,616 generations), and we compare every edit against a byte-identical re-run of the same prompt.\nA single invisible edit flips 4.9\\% of classification labels (95\\% CI 4.6--5.1\\%), while the identical re-run flips 0.02\\%.\nContrary to our hypothesis, whitespace edits flip 7.1\\% of labels and punctuation edits only 2.2\\%. Whitespace is more disruptive for every model and on both task types, and it stays more disruptive when we match the number of prompt tokens the edit changes.\nModels under 3B parameters flip labels 2.2$\\times$ as often as larger ones (CI 2.0--2.4).\nFor code, 36\\% of generations change text under an edit, but only 5.1\\% change their unit-test outcome, about the same rate as classification label flips.\nNet accuracy moves by less than 1 percentage point per model, because flips go in both directions and concentrate on items the model already gets wrong.\nFormatting therefore adds variance rather than bias. Practitioners should normalise whitespace before inference and report results over several formattings.\nEOF\ncat > sections/introduction.tex <<'EOF'\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nTwo prompts can look the same to a person and still differ in bytes.\nA review pasted from a web page carries no-break spaces (U+00A0) instead of ordinary spaces.\nA file saved on Windows ends its lines with \\texttt{\\textbackslash r\\textbackslash n}.\nA word processor replaces straight quotes with curly ones and hyphens with dashes.\nNone of these edits changes the meaning of the text, and most are invisible on screen.\nOur main question is simple: {\\bf do these invisible edits change what a language model outputs?}\n\nThe answer matters for anyone who measures or deploys LLMs.\nIf invisible formatting changes outputs, then benchmark numbers contain a hidden formatting term, A/B tests of prompt variants are confounded, and the same input behaves differently depending on the editor or operating system that produced it.\nSmall on-device models are a particular concern, because practitioners often assume they are brittle but rarely know by how much.\n\nPrior work shows that LLMs are sensitive to prompt design.\nChanging separators, casing or field names in a few-shot template can move accuracy by tens of points~\\cite{sclar2024quantifying}, single-prompt evaluations give unstable rankings~\\cite{mizrahi2024state}, and sensitivity varies across models and tasks~\\cite{zhuo2024prosa}.\nHowever, these studies perturb \\emph{visible} template structure.\nCharacter-level robustness work studies typos and adversarial edits~\\cite{belinkov2018synthetic,pruthi2019combating,wang2021textflint}, which a reader can also see.\nWe lack a controlled comparison of edits that a reader cannot see, one that separates whitespace from punctuation, spans model sizes within families, covers both classification and code, and measures each effect against the noise floor of inference itself.\n\nWe fill this gap with a controlled study.\nWe define five whitespace edits (trailing spaces, CRLF line endings, double spaces, trailing newlines, and no-break spaces or tabs) and five punctuation edits (curly quotes, curly apostrophes, Unicode dashes, the ellipsis character, and all four combined).\nWe apply each edit to every item of \\sst, \\agnews, \\boolq, \\humaneval and \\mbpp, and we run 11 open instruction-tuned models from the \\qwentwo, \\qwenthree and \\llama families, ranging from 0.5B to 14.8B parameters.\nEvery perturbed output is compared with the same model's output on the canonical prompt.\nEvery item also gets a \\emph{control} condition: the byte-identical canonical prompt placed at another batch position, which measures how often outputs change for no reason at all.\nFinally, we pass every prompt through each model's chat template and tokenizer to check whether the edit survives and how many tokens it changes.\n\n\\Figref{fig:per_condition} summarizes the main result.\nA single invisible edit flips 4.9\\% of classification labels on average, more than 200 times the 0.02\\% control rate.\nWe expected punctuation to matter more than whitespace. The opposite holds: whitespace edits flip 7.1\\% of labels and punctuation edits 2.2\\%, and whitespace is worse for all 11 models.\nPart of the gap comes from token footprint, because a double-space or no-break-space edit re-tokenizes every word. Even at a matched footprint, though, whitespace is worse: a trailing \\texttt{\\textbackslash n\\textbackslash n} (about one changed token) flips 3.4\\% of labels, while a Unicode dash (1.3 tokens) flips 1.3\\%.\nModels under 3B parameters flip 2.2$\\times$ as often as larger models on classification, but code shows no size effect.\nCode text changes in 36\\% of generations, yet only 5.1\\% of programs change their test outcome.\n\nOur contributions are:\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,topsep=0pt]\n    \\item We propose a taxonomy of ten invisible formatting edits, split into whitespace and punctuation families, with code-safe variants that leave programs valid.\n    \\item We conduct a controlled study over 11 open models, 5 tasks and 239,616 greedy generations, with a byte-identical re-run as the noise floor and an exact replication in a separate engine.\n    \\item We show that whitespace edits are 2--5$\\times$ more disruptive than punctuation edits, that small models are about 2$\\times$ more label-sensitive, and that most code changes are stylistic rather than functional.\n    \\item We trace the effect to the token level. Edits that the chat template removes, such as trailing newlines under \\llama templates, have zero effect, and divergence grows with the number of changed tokens without fully explaining the whitespace--punctuation gap.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\Secref{sec:related} reviews related work, \\secref{sec:method} describes our setup, \\secref{sec:results} reports results, and \\secref{sec:discussion} discusses mechanisms, implications and limitations.\nEOF","description":"Write abstract and introduction"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01Wnre7a2tvs9cGwYkPuYDFz":{"cwd":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/invisible_formatting_effects_20261029_120001_qr78st90/paper_draft"}}}
