{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgXFFmcMbHSZ3GJonke9e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Next, the related work and methodology sections."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2445,"cache_read_input_tokens":71630,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2445},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a7259184-e90b-461a-a0ed-e4b7ad9b7a2d","uuid":"cf286fd9-a206-4e8e-ab47-d6c7ddc188bb","timestamp":"2026-10-29T21:34:44.479Z","request_id":"req_011CgXFFmGkmCrZq9j9PWAUR"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgXFFmcMbHSZ3GJonke9e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01717h9NBeLiLujWBpvTnqgb","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat > sections/related_work.tex <<'EOF'\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\para{Prompt-format sensitivity.}\nA growing body of work shows that LLM outputs depend on prompt details that should not matter.\n\\citet{zhao2021calibrate} and \\citet{lu2022fantastically} show that the choice and order of few-shot examples can swing accuracy from near chance to near state of the art.\n\\citet{sclar2024quantifying} vary separators, casing and spacing between template fields and find accuracy differences of up to 76 points for LLaMA-2-13B.\n\\citet{mizrahi2024state} show that model rankings change across paraphrased instructions and argue for multi-prompt evaluation.\n\\citet{zhuo2024prosa} measure instance-level prompt sensitivity across models and link it to model confidence.\nThese studies perturb \\emph{visible} structure that a prompt designer chooses.\nUnlike them, we hold the visible prompt fixed and change only characters a reader cannot see or barely notices, and we separate whitespace from punctuation edits.\n\n\\para{Character-level robustness.}\nNeural NLP models are known to be fragile under character noise.\n\\citet{belinkov2018synthetic} show that synthetic and natural typos break machine translation, \\citet{pruthi2019combating} show that single-character adversarial edits fool text classifiers, and toolkits such as TextFlint~\\cite{wang2021textflint} bundle many such transformations.\nThese edits are visible typos or adversarially chosen changes.\nOur edits are neither: they are the benign byte-level variation that ordinary software introduces, and none of them changes how the text reads.\n\n\\para{Tokenization.}\nByte-level BPE tokenizers~\\cite{sennrich2016neural,radford2019language} fold a leading space into the following word token, so \\texttt{\"word\"} and \\texttt{\"~word\"} map to different ids, and an unusual space character changes the token of every word it precedes.\nThis suggests a mechanism for whitespace sensitivity.\nWe measure it directly by applying each model's chat template and tokenizer and counting the token positions an edit changes.\nWe find that this token footprint explains part, but not all, of the gap between whitespace and punctuation.\n\n\\para{Nondeterminism of inference.}\nGreedy decoding is often treated as deterministic, but batched GPU inference with engines such as vLLM~\\cite{kwon2023efficient} can change outputs depending on batch composition, because floating-point reductions are not associative~\\cite{atil2024llm}.\nAny sensitivity study that ignores this floor attributes noise to the perturbation.\nWe include a byte-identical control condition for every item and report all effects against it.\nOn code generation, this floor alone changes 6.2\\% of outputs.\nEOF\ncat > sections/methodology.tex <<'EOF'\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\subsection{Problem formulation}\nLet $f_m$ be model $m$ with greedy decoding and let $x_i$ be the canonical prompt for item $i$.\nA perturbation $p$ is a string edit that preserves meaning and is invisible or nearly invisible when rendered.\nIt produces $p(x_i)$.\nWe measure divergence relative to the model's own canonical answer, not relative to the gold label:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\label{eq:div}\nD_m(p) = \\frac{1}{|\\gI_p|}\\sum_{i \\in \\gI_p} \\1\\big[g(f_m(p(x_i))) \\neq g(f_m(x_i))\\big],\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $g$ maps a raw output to the quantity of interest (a parsed label, the extracted code, or a unit-test outcome) and $\\gI_p = \\{i : p(x_i) \\neq x_i\\}$ is the set of items the edit actually changes.\nThe \\ctrl condition sets $p$ to the identity but places the prompt at a different position in the batch, so $D_m(\\ctrl)$ is the noise floor of inference.\n\n\\subsection{Perturbations}\n\\Tabref{tab:perturbations} lists the edits.\nThe whitespace family has four edits shared by all tasks, plus no-break spaces (\\cond{nbsp}) for classification or tab indentation (\\cond{tabs}) for code.\nThe punctuation family replaces ASCII quotes, apostrophes, dashes and ellipses with their typographic Unicode forms, separately and all together (\\cond{punct_all}).\nWe wrote the task instructions to contain double quotes, an apostrophe (``Don't'') and a spaced hyphen, so that every punctuation edit changes every prompt.\nThe exception is \\cond{ellipsis_char}, which applies only to the 40 classification and 9 code prompts that contain \\texttt{...}.\nEdits that leave a prompt unchanged are excluded from all rates.\n\nCode prompts must stay valid Python.\nWe apply \\cond{double_space} and all punctuation edits only to natural language: the instruction, docstrings and comments, which we locate with Python's \\texttt{tokenize} module.\nDoctest lines (\\texttt{>>>} and their expected output) are left untouched.\nLayout edits (\\cond{trailing_space}, \\cond{crlf}, \\cond{tabs}, \\cond{trailing_newlines}) apply to the whole message.\n\n\\input{tables/perturbations}\n\n\\subsection{Tasks and models}\n\\para{Classification.}\nWe sample 400 items each (seed 42) from the GLUE~\\cite{wang2018glue} validation split of \\sst~\\cite{socher2013recursive}, the test split of \\agnews~\\cite{zhang2015character} and the validation split of \\boolq~\\cite{clark2019boolq} (passages of at most 180 words).\nEach prompt is a zero-shot instruction such as \\texttt{Classify the sentiment of the movie review below as \"positive\" or \"negative\".} followed by \\texttt{Don't explain - answer with one word only.} and the input.\nWe parse the earliest label keyword in the output, map synonyms, and assign \\texttt{other} to anything else.\n\n\\para{Code generation.}\nWe use all 164 \\humaneval problems~\\cite{chen2021evaluating} and the 300-problem \\mbpp test split~\\cite{austin2021program}.\n\\humaneval prompts ask the model to complete the given function and return it in one fenced block. \\mbpp prompts give the task text and its three \\texttt{assert} tests.\nWe extract the first fenced block that contains a \\texttt{def} and normalize CRLF and trailing whitespace.\nWe then run the unit tests in an isolated subprocess (\\texttt{python -I}, 2\\,GB memory limit, 20\\,s timeout).\n\n\\para{Models.}\nWe run 11 open instruction-tuned models: \\qwentwo 0.5B, 1.5B, 3B, 7B and 14B~\\cite{qwen2025qwen25}; \\qwenthree 0.6B, 4B and 8B~\\cite{yang2025qwen3}, with thinking mode disabled; and Llama-3.2 1B and 3B and Llama-3.1 8B~\\cite{grattafiori2024llama3}.\nMost checkpoints are AWQ 4-bit~\\cite{lin2024awq}. Llama-3.2-1B, \\qwenthree-0.6B and \\qwenthree-8B run in bf16.\nAll models run in vLLM 0.32~\\cite{kwon2023efficient} on NVIDIA RTX A6000 GPUs with temperature 0, using at most 12 new tokens for classification and 512 for code.\nWe call the four models under 3B parameters \\emph{small} and the other seven \\emph{large}.\nEach model sees 19,968 prompts (1,664 items $\\times$ 12 conditions), for 239,616 generations in total including a replication run.\nWe had no commercial API access, so the study covers open-weight models only.\n\n\\subsection{Metrics}\nWe report four divergence metrics, each an instance of \\eqref{eq:div}:\n(1) \\textbf{label flip}, where the parsed classification label differs;\n(2) \\textbf{code changed}, where the normalized extracted code differs;\n(3) \\textbf{pass/fail flip}, where the unit-test outcome differs; and\n(4) \\textbf{raw text changed}, where the output string differs.\nWe also report accuracy against gold labels and tests.\nFor every model and prompt, we apply the chat template and tokenizer and record whether the token sequence changes and how many positions differ (\\dtok, computed with \\texttt{difflib}).\n\n\\subsection{Statistical analysis}\nAll metrics are paired at the item level.\nWe compute 95\\% confidence intervals with a bootstrap over items (2,000 resamples). For the small/large ratio, the bootstrap resamples the same items for all models.\nFor each of the 110 model $\\times$ condition cells, we test perturbation against control with an exact McNemar test on discordant items and apply Holm correction~\\cite{holm1979simple}.\nWe fit GEE logistic models~\\cite{liang1986longitudinal} with exchangeable correlation clustered by item, of the form flip $\\sim$ punctuation + small + task, with and without $\\log(1+\\Delta\\text{tok})$, using statsmodels~\\cite{seabold2010statsmodels}.\n\n\\para{Reproducibility checks.}\nWe re-ran \\qwentwo-0.5B in a separate engine process on a different GPU. All 19,968 outputs were byte-identical to the first run, so every rate for that model replicates exactly.\nLlama-3.x chat templates strip trailing whitespace from messages, so \\cond{trailing_newlines} leaves their token sequence unchanged. This gives a natural placebo: an edit to the raw string that the model never sees.\nEOF\ncat > tables/perturbations.tex <<'EOF'\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\small\n    \\resizebox{\\textwidth}{!}{%\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{8.4cm}l@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        \\textbf{Family} & \\textbf{Condition} & \\textbf{Edit} & \\textbf{Applies to} \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Control & \\ctrl & Byte-identical to canonical, different batch position & all \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        \\multirow{6}{*}{Whitespace} & \\cond{trailing_space} & Append a space to every line & all \\\\\n        & \\cond{crlf} & Replace \\texttt{\\textbackslash n} with \\texttt{\\textbackslash r\\textbackslash n} & all \\\\\n        & \\cond{double_space} & Replace every single inter-word space with two spaces & all (code: prose only) \\\\\n        & \\cond{trailing_newlines} & Append \\texttt{\\textbackslash n\\textbackslash n} to the message & all \\\\\n        & \\cond{nbsp} & Replace inter-word spaces with no-break space U+00A0 & classification \\\\\n        & \\cond{tabs} & Replace 4-space indentation with a tab & \\humaneval \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        \\multirow{5}{*}{Punctuation} & \\cond{curly_quotes} & \\texttt{\"x\"} $\\rightarrow$ \\textquotedblleft x\\textquotedblright & all (code: prose only) \\\\\n        & \\cond{curly_apostrophe} & \\texttt{'} $\\rightarrow$ \\textquoteright\\ / \\textquoteleft & all (code: prose only) \\\\\n        & \\cond{unicode_dash} & \\texttt{~-~} $\\rightarrow$ en dash, \\texttt{--} $\\rightarrow$ em dash, digit ranges $\\rightarrow$ en dash & all (code: prose only) \\\\\n        & \\cond{ellipsis_char} & \\texttt{...} $\\rightarrow$ U+2026 (\\ldots) & 40 cls / 9 code prompts \\\\\n        & \\cond{punct_all} & All four punctuation edits combined & all (code: prose only) \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    }\n    \\caption{The ten invisible formatting edits plus the identical-prompt control. ``Prose only'' means the edit touches the instruction, docstrings and comments but never code tokens or doctest lines, so programs stay valid.}\n    \\label{tab:perturbations}\n\\end{table}\nEOF","description":"Write related work, methodology, and perturbation table"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2445,"cache_read_input_tokens":71630,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2445},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a7259184-e90b-461a-a0ed-e4b7ad9b7a2d","uuid":"231966f6-a413-4ddd-a906-023edcbcd5e2","timestamp":"2026-10-29T21:35:19.561Z","request_id":"req_011CgXFFmGkmCrZq9j9PWAUR","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01717h9NBeLiLujWBpvTnqgb":{"command":"cd /data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/invisible_formatting_effects_20261029_120001_qr78st90/paper_draft && cat > sections/related_work.tex <<'EOF'\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\para{Prompt-format sensitivity.}\nA growing body of work shows that LLM outputs depend on prompt details that should not matter.\n\\citet{zhao2021calibrate} and \\citet{lu2022fantastically} show that the choice and order of few-shot examples can swing accuracy from near chance to near state of the art.\n\\citet{sclar2024quantifying} vary separators, casing and spacing between template fields and find accuracy differences of up to 76 points for LLaMA-2-13B.\n\\citet{mizrahi2024state} show that model rankings change across paraphrased instructions and argue for multi-prompt evaluation.\n\\citet{zhuo2024prosa} measure instance-level prompt sensitivity across models and link it to model confidence.\nThese studies perturb \\emph{visible} structure that a prompt designer chooses.\nUnlike them, we hold the visible prompt fixed and change only characters a reader cannot see or barely notices, and we separate whitespace from punctuation edits.\n\n\\para{Character-level robustness.}\nNeural NLP models are known to be fragile under character noise.\n\\citet{belinkov2018synthetic} show that synthetic and natural typos break machine translation, \\citet{pruthi2019combating} show that single-character adversarial edits fool text classifiers, and toolkits such as TextFlint~\\cite{wang2021textflint} bundle many such transformations.\nThese edits are visible typos or adversarially chosen changes.\nOur edits are neither: they are the benign byte-level variation that ordinary software introduces, and none of them changes how the text reads.\n\n\\para{Tokenization.}\nByte-level BPE tokenizers~\\cite{sennrich2016neural,radford2019language} fold a leading space into the following word token, so \\texttt{\"word\"} and \\texttt{\"~word\"} map to different ids, and an unusual space character changes the token of every word it precedes.\nThis suggests a mechanism for whitespace sensitivity.\nWe measure it directly by applying each model's chat template and tokenizer and counting the token positions an edit changes.\nWe find that this token footprint explains part, but not all, of the gap between whitespace and punctuation.\n\n\\para{Nondeterminism of inference.}\nGreedy decoding is often treated as deterministic, but batched GPU inference with engines such as vLLM~\\cite{kwon2023efficient} can change outputs depending on batch composition, because floating-point reductions are not associative~\\cite{atil2024llm}.\nAny sensitivity study that ignores this floor attributes noise to the perturbation.\nWe include a byte-identical control condition for every item and report all effects against it.\nOn code generation, this floor alone changes 6.2\\% of outputs.\nEOF\ncat > sections/methodology.tex <<'EOF'\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\subsection{Problem formulation}\nLet $f_m$ be model $m$ with greedy decoding and let $x_i$ be the canonical prompt for item $i$.\nA perturbation $p$ is a string edit that preserves meaning and is invisible or nearly invisible when rendered.\nIt produces $p(x_i)$.\nWe measure divergence relative to the model's own canonical answer, not relative to the gold label:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\label{eq:div}\nD_m(p) = \\frac{1}{|\\gI_p|}\\sum_{i \\in \\gI_p} \\1\\big[g(f_m(p(x_i))) \\neq g(f_m(x_i))\\big],\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $g$ maps a raw output to the quantity of interest (a parsed label, the extracted code, or a unit-test outcome) and $\\gI_p = \\{i : p(x_i) \\neq x_i\\}$ is the set of items the edit actually changes.\nThe \\ctrl condition sets $p$ to the identity but places the prompt at a different position in the batch, so $D_m(\\ctrl)$ is the noise floor of inference.\n\n\\subsection{Perturbations}\n\\Tabref{tab:perturbations} lists the edits.\nThe whitespace family has four edits shared by all tasks, plus no-break spaces (\\cond{nbsp}) for classification or tab indentation (\\cond{tabs}) for code.\nThe punctuation family replaces ASCII quotes, apostrophes, dashes and ellipses with their typographic Unicode forms, separately and all together (\\cond{punct_all}).\nWe wrote the task instructions to contain double quotes, an apostrophe (``Don't'') and a spaced hyphen, so that every punctuation edit changes every prompt.\nThe exception is \\cond{ellipsis_char}, which applies only to the 40 classification and 9 code prompts that contain \\texttt{...}.\nEdits that leave a prompt unchanged are excluded from all rates.\n\nCode prompts must stay valid Python.\nWe apply \\cond{double_space} and all punctuation edits only to natural language: the instruction, docstrings and comments, which we locate with Python's \\texttt{tokenize} module.\nDoctest lines (\\texttt{>>>} and their expected output) are left untouched.\nLayout edits (\\cond{trailing_space}, \\cond{crlf}, \\cond{tabs}, \\cond{trailing_newlines}) apply to the whole message.\n\n\\input{tables/perturbations}\n\n\\subsection{Tasks and models}\n\\para{Classification.}\nWe sample 400 items each (seed 42) from the GLUE~\\cite{wang2018glue} validation split of \\sst~\\cite{socher2013recursive}, the test split of \\agnews~\\cite{zhang2015character} and the validation split of \\boolq~\\cite{clark2019boolq} (passages of at most 180 words).\nEach prompt is a zero-shot instruction such as \\texttt{Classify the sentiment of the movie review below as \"positive\" or \"negative\".} followed by \\texttt{Don't explain - answer with one word only.} and the input.\nWe parse the earliest label keyword in the output, map synonyms, and assign \\texttt{other} to anything else.\n\n\\para{Code generation.}\nWe use all 164 \\humaneval problems~\\cite{chen2021evaluating} and the 300-problem \\mbpp test split~\\cite{austin2021program}.\n\\humaneval prompts ask the model to complete the given function and return it in one fenced block. \\mbpp prompts give the task text and its three \\texttt{assert} tests.\nWe extract the first fenced block that contains a \\texttt{def} and normalize CRLF and trailing whitespace.\nWe then run the unit tests in an isolated subprocess (\\texttt{python -I}, 2\\,GB memory limit, 20\\,s timeout).\n\n\\para{Models.}\nWe run 11 open instruction-tuned models: \\qwentwo 0.5B, 1.5B, 3B, 7B and 14B~\\cite{qwen2025qwen25}; \\qwenthree 0.6B, 4B and 8B~\\cite{yang2025qwen3}, with thinking mode disabled; and Llama-3.2 1B and 3B and Llama-3.1 8B~\\cite{grattafiori2024llama3}.\nMost checkpoints are AWQ 4-bit~\\cite{lin2024awq}. Llama-3.2-1B, \\qwenthree-0.6B and \\qwenthree-8B run in bf16.\nAll models run in vLLM 0.32~\\cite{kwon2023efficient} on NVIDIA RTX A6000 GPUs with temperature 0, using at most 12 new tokens for classification and 512 for code.\nWe call the four models under 3B parameters \\emph{small} and the other seven \\emph{large}.\nEach model sees 19,968 prompts (1,664 items $\\times$ 12 conditions), for 239,616 generations in total including a replication run.\nWe had no commercial API access, so the study covers open-weight models only.\n\n\\subsection{Metrics}\nWe report four divergence metrics, each an instance of \\eqref{eq:div}:\n(1) \\textbf{label flip}, where the parsed classification label differs;\n(2) \\textbf{code changed}, where the normalized extracted code differs;\n(3) \\textbf{pass/fail flip}, where the unit-test outcome differs; and\n(4) \\textbf{raw text changed}, where the output string differs.\nWe also report accuracy against gold labels and tests.\nFor every model and prompt, we apply the chat template and tokenizer and record whether the token sequence changes and how many positions differ (\\dtok, computed with \\texttt{difflib}).\n\n\\subsection{Statistical analysis}\nAll metrics are paired at the item level.\nWe compute 95\\% confidence intervals with a bootstrap over items (2,000 resamples). For the small/large ratio, the bootstrap resamples the same items for all models.\nFor each of the 110 model $\\times$ condition cells, we test perturbation against control with an exact McNemar test on discordant items and apply Holm correction~\\cite{holm1979simple}.\nWe fit GEE logistic models~\\cite{liang1986longitudinal} with exchangeable correlation clustered by item, of the form flip $\\sim$ punctuation + small + task, with and without $\\log(1+\\Delta\\text{tok})$, using statsmodels~\\cite{seabold2010statsmodels}.\n\n\\para{Reproducibility checks.}\nWe re-ran \\qwentwo-0.5B in a separate engine process on a different GPU. All 19,968 outputs were byte-identical to the first run, so every rate for that model replicates exactly.\nLlama-3.x chat templates strip trailing whitespace from messages, so \\cond{trailing_newlines} leaves their token sequence unchanged. This gives a natural placebo: an edit to the raw string that the model never sees.\nEOF\ncat > tables/perturbations.tex <<'EOF'\n\\begin{table}[t]\n    \\centering\n    \\small\n    \\resizebox{\\textwidth}{!}{%\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}llp{8.4cm}l@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        \\textbf{Family} & \\textbf{Condition} & \\textbf{Edit} & \\textbf{Applies to} \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        Control & \\ctrl & Byte-identical to canonical, different batch position & all \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        \\multirow{6}{*}{Whitespace} & \\cond{trailing_space} & Append a space to every line & all \\\\\n        & \\cond{crlf} & Replace \\texttt{\\textbackslash n} with \\texttt{\\textbackslash r\\textbackslash n} & all \\\\\n        & \\cond{double_space} & Replace every single inter-word space with two spaces & all (code: prose only) \\\\\n        & \\cond{trailing_newlines} & Append \\texttt{\\textbackslash n\\textbackslash n} to the message & all \\\\\n        & \\cond{nbsp} & Replace inter-word spaces with no-break space U+00A0 & classification \\\\\n        & \\cond{tabs} & Replace 4-space indentation with a tab & \\humaneval \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        \\multirow{5}{*}{Punctuation} & \\cond{curly_quotes} & \\texttt{\"x\"} $\\rightarrow$ \\textquotedblleft x\\textquotedblright & all (code: prose only) \\\\\n        & \\cond{curly_apostrophe} & \\texttt{'} $\\rightarrow$ \\textquoteright\\ / \\textquoteleft & all (code: prose only) \\\\\n        & \\cond{unicode_dash} & \\texttt{~-~} $\\rightarrow$ en dash, \\texttt{--} $\\rightarrow$ em dash, digit ranges $\\rightarrow$ en dash & all (code: prose only) \\\\\n        & \\cond{ellipsis_char} & \\texttt{...} $\\rightarrow$ U+2026 (\\ldots) & 40 cls / 9 code prompts \\\\\n        & \\cond{punct_all} & All four punctuation edits combined & all (code: prose only) \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    }\n    \\caption{The ten invisible formatting edits plus the identical-prompt control. ``Prose only'' means the edit touches the instruction, docstrings and comments but never code tokens or doctest lines, so programs stay valid.}\n    \\label{tab:perturbations}\n\\end{table}\nEOF","description":"Write related work, methodology, and perturbation table"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01717h9NBeLiLujWBpvTnqgb":{"cwd":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/invisible_formatting_effects_20261029_120001_qr78st90/paper_draft"}}}
