{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgXFNwqwcHS83c1VR5EJz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KZE2dTZLgiS3V44FRhboV6","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -i 's/For classification flips, the whitespace \\$-\\$ punctuation difference is \\$-4.9\\$\\\\pp \\\\ci{\\$-5.2\\$}{\\$-4.6\\$} (punctuation minus whitespace)./For classification flips, the punctuation $-$ whitespace difference is $-4.9$\\\\pp \\\\ci{$-5.2$}{$-4.6$}./' tables/overall.tex && grep -n \"difference\" tables/overall.tex\ncat > sections/discussion.tex <<'EOF'\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n\n\\para{Hypotheses revisited.}\nWe started with four hypotheses.\n(H1) Invisible edits flip 5--15\\% of labels. This is \\emph{borderline}. The mean is 4.9\\% \\ci{4.6}{5.1}, just below the range. Small models fall inside it (7.5\\%) and large models below it (3.4\\%), and individual cells range from 0\\% to 39\\%.\n(H2) Punctuation edits are more disruptive than whitespace edits. This is \\emph{refuted}. Whitespace edits flip 7.1\\% of labels, inside the predicted 3--8\\%, while punctuation edits flip only 2.2\\%, far below the predicted 8--20\\%.\n(H3) Models under 3B are 2--3$\\times$ more sensitive. This is \\emph{supported for classification} (2.2$\\times$ overall, 2.9$\\times$ within \\qwentwo) but \\emph{not for code} (1.14$\\times$, not significant).\n(H4) Code is most affected, with 15--25\\% output change. This is \\emph{partly supported}. Code text changes more than predicted (36\\%), but functional flips (5.1\\%) match classification label flips (4.9\\%).\n\n{\\bf Why is whitespace worse than punctuation?}\nWe see two mechanisms.\nThe first is token footprint.\nByte-level BPE tokenizers attach the leading space to the next word, so a double space or a no-break space changes the token of every word in the prompt (69 and 148 changed positions on average), while curly quotes touch a handful of tokens.\nThe second mechanism operates even at a matched footprint.\nA trailing \\texttt{\\textbackslash n\\textbackslash n} changes the last tokens before the assistant turn, the position that most directly conditions the first answer token.\nCRLF changes every line boundary the model uses to parse the prompt's structure.\nTypographic quotes and dashes, by contrast, are common in web pretraining text, and models appear to treat them as near-synonyms of their ASCII forms.\nThe GEE results support this reading: controlling for edit size shrinks the punctuation--whitespace gap but does not close it.\n\n{\\bf Why is code text so volatile but code correctness not?}\nA 512-token program has many places where an equally valid choice can differ, such as a variable name, a loop form or a blank line.\nGreedy decoding commits to one branch, and a small change in the prompt can tip an early near-tie.\nMost of these alternatives are equally correct, so 31\\% of perturbed generations change text without changing test outcomes.\nThe same reasoning explains the larger code noise floor: re-running an identical prompt in a different batch position already changes 6.2\\% of code outputs, up to 21\\% for \\qwenthree-0.6B, but only 0.02\\% of labels.\nWithout this control, about a sixth of the code-change effect would be misattributed to formatting.\n\n\\para{Implications for practice.}\nFirst, whitespace normalization matters more than punctuation normalization. Converting \\texttt{\\textbackslash r\\textbackslash n} to \\texttt{\\textbackslash n}, replacing no-break spaces with spaces, collapsing repeated spaces and stripping trailing whitespace removes the most disruptive edits we tested.\nSecond, a 2--5 point accuracy difference between two prompt variants on a small model can come from formatting alone. Evaluations should report results over several formattings and compare against an identical-prompt re-run.\nThird, chat templates silently normalize some formatting (Llama trims trailing whitespace) and not other formatting (\\qwentwo keeps it), so the same raw input can be safe for one serving stack and fragile for another.\nFinally, because net accuracy barely moves, formatting is a source of variance in individual predictions rather than a systematic bias. This matters most for per-item consistency, such as when the same document is classified twice through different pipelines.\n\n\\para{Limitations.}\nWe test only open models up to 14.8B parameters, since no commercial API was available, and frontier models may behave differently.\nMost checkpoints are AWQ 4-bit, and quantization may itself add sensitivity. bf16 \\qwenthree-8B is the least sensitive model, but it also differs in family and was served by a separate vLLM server, which raises its control noise.\nWe use greedy decoding only, so we measure the deterministic component of the effect, not shifts in the sampling distribution.\nThe edit set is a design choice. Two whitespace edits (\\cond{double_space}, \\cond{nbsp}) touch every word. Without them, whitespace flips 4.5\\% of labels (mean of \\cond{trailing_space}, \\cond{crlf} and \\cond{trailing_newlines}), still about twice the punctuation rate.\nPunctuation edits can only change the few quotes and dashes present, and a quote-heavy corpus could show larger effects. \\cond{ellipsis_char} rarely applied.\nIn code prompts, punctuation edits touch only docstrings and comments, so they are smaller than in classification prompts.\nNear-constant predictions from Llama-3.2-1B on \\boolq and \\qwenthree-0.6B on \\agnews understate small-model sensitivity on those tasks.\nFinally, we use one prompt template per task, 400 items per classification task and 464 code problems, and other templates may be more or less fragile.\n\n\\para{Broader impact.}\nOur findings help practitioners build more reliable evaluations and pipelines. They could also inform adversarial manipulation of model outputs through invisible characters.\nBecause the effects we observe are mostly symmetric and concentrated on uncertain items, simple input normalization is an effective defence.\nEOF\ncat > sections/conclusion.tex <<'EOF'\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe study whether invisible, meaning-preserving formatting edits change LLM outputs.\nAcross 11 open models, five tasks and 239,616 greedy generations, a single invisible edit flips about 5\\% of classification labels (7.5\\% for models under 3B, 3.4\\% for larger ones), more than 200 times the rate of an identical re-run.\nContrary to our hypothesis, whitespace edits (no-break spaces, double spaces, CRLF, trailing spaces and newlines) are 2--5$\\times$ more disruptive than typographic punctuation, and token footprint explains only part of this gap.\nCode text changes in 36\\% of generations, but code correctness flips about as often as classification labels, and net accuracy barely moves.\nThe key takeaway is that \\emph{whitespace is not neutral}: normalize it before inference, and evaluate prompts under several formattings against an identical-prompt baseline.\n\nFuture work should test frontier API models and design matched-token-budget edits that separate \\emph{what} changes from \\emph{how many} tokens change.\nOther directions include logit-margin analysis to test whether flips occur only when the top two labels are close, measuring distribution shift under sampling rather than greedy flips, and studying whitespace inside code bodies for code-repair tasks.\nEOF\ncat > appendix/details.tex <<'EOF'\n\\section{Additional Experimental Details}\n\\label{app:details}\n\n\\para{Model checkpoints.}\n\\Tabref{tab:models} lists the exact checkpoints.\nAll local models run in vLLM 0.32 on shared RTX A6000 GPUs. \\qwenthree-8B is served by a separate vLLM instance through its OpenAI-compatible API.\nGeneration took about three hours in total, from 3--10 minutes for 0.5--1.5B models to about 1h40 for the 14B model.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\small\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}llcc@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        \\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Checkpoint} & \\textbf{Params (B)} & \\textbf{Precision} \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        \\qwentwo-\\{0.5,1.5,3,7,14\\}B & \\texttt{Qwen/Qwen2.5-\\{\\ldots\\}-Instruct-AWQ} & 0.5--14.8 & AWQ-INT4 \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-1B & \\texttt{unsloth/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct} & 1.2 & bf16 \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-3B & \\texttt{AMead10/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct-AWQ} & 3.2 & AWQ \\\\\n        Llama-3.1-8B & \\texttt{hugging-quants/Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-AWQ-INT4} & 8.0 & AWQ \\\\\n        \\qwenthree-0.6B & \\texttt{Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B} (thinking off) & 0.6 & bf16 \\\\\n        \\qwenthree-4B & \\texttt{Qwen/Qwen3-4B-AWQ} (thinking off) & 4.0 & AWQ \\\\\n        \\qwenthree-8B & \\texttt{Qwen/Qwen3-8B} via vLLM server (thinking off) & 8.2 & bf16 \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    \\caption{Model checkpoints. Small models ($<$3B) are \\qwentwo-0.5B, \\qwentwo-1.5B, Llama-3.2-1B and \\qwenthree-0.6B.}\n    \\label{tab:models}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\para{Prompt templates.}\nThe classification instructions are:\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,topsep=2pt]\n    \\item \\sst: \\texttt{Classify the sentiment of the movie review below as \"positive\" or \"negative\".} \\texttt{Don't explain - answer with one word only.} \\texttt{Review: \\ldots{} Sentiment:}\n    \\item \\agnews: the same format with the four topics \\texttt{\"World\", \"Sports\", \"Business\" or \"Sci/Tech\"}.\n    \\item \\boolq: \\texttt{Read the passage and answer the question with \"yes\" or \"no\".} followed by the same answer-format line, the passage and the question.\n\\end{itemize}\nFor \\humaneval, the prompt reads: \\texttt{Complete the following Python function. Don't change its signature - return the complete function \\ldots{} in a single \\`{}\\`{}\\`{}python code block, without \"explanations\" or tests.}, followed by the function stub.\nFor \\mbpp, the prompt contains the task text, \\texttt{Your code should pass these tests:} and the three asserts.\nThe double quotes, the apostrophe in ``Don't'' and the spaced hyphen ensure that every punctuation edit except \\cond{ellipsis_char} changes every prompt.\n\n\\para{Code-pass/fail flips by edit size.}\n\\Figref{fig:heat_code} shows the per-model pass/fail flip rate for code.\nAs with classification, whitespace columns dominate, especially \\cond{double_space} and \\cond{tabs}.\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.8\\linewidth]{figures/heat_code_flip.png}\n    \\caption{Code pass/fail flip rate (\\%) per model and edit, pooled over \\humaneval and \\mbpp. Whitespace edits produce the largest functional changes across models.}\n    \\label{fig:heat_code}\n\\end{figure}\nEOF","description":"Write discussion, conclusion, appendix"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7291,"cache_read_input_tokens":78896,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7291},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a7259184-e90b-461a-a0ed-e4b7ad9b7a2d","uuid":"df29942b-77ff-4828-ab9b-718413f3ac63","timestamp":"2026-10-29T21:36:53.669Z","request_id":"req_011CgXFNwf3MRs1UevDyKhw7","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01KZE2dTZLgiS3V44FRhboV6":{"command":"cd /data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/invisible_formatting_effects_20261029_120001_qr78st90/paper_draft && sed -i 's/For classification flips, the whitespace \\$-\\$ punctuation difference is \\$-4.9\\$\\\\pp \\\\ci{\\$-5.2\\$}{\\$-4.6\\$} (punctuation minus whitespace)./For classification flips, the punctuation $-$ whitespace difference is $-4.9$\\\\pp \\\\ci{$-5.2$}{$-4.6$}./' tables/overall.tex && grep -n \"difference\" tables/overall.tex\ncat > sections/discussion.tex <<'EOF'\n\\section{Discussion}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n\n\\para{Hypotheses revisited.}\nWe started with four hypotheses.\n(H1) Invisible edits flip 5--15\\% of labels. This is \\emph{borderline}. The mean is 4.9\\% \\ci{4.6}{5.1}, just below the range. Small models fall inside it (7.5\\%) and large models below it (3.4\\%), and individual cells range from 0\\% to 39\\%.\n(H2) Punctuation edits are more disruptive than whitespace edits. This is \\emph{refuted}. Whitespace edits flip 7.1\\% of labels, inside the predicted 3--8\\%, while punctuation edits flip only 2.2\\%, far below the predicted 8--20\\%.\n(H3) Models under 3B are 2--3$\\times$ more sensitive. This is \\emph{supported for classification} (2.2$\\times$ overall, 2.9$\\times$ within \\qwentwo) but \\emph{not for code} (1.14$\\times$, not significant).\n(H4) Code is most affected, with 15--25\\% output change. This is \\emph{partly supported}. Code text changes more than predicted (36\\%), but functional flips (5.1\\%) match classification label flips (4.9\\%).\n\n{\\bf Why is whitespace worse than punctuation?}\nWe see two mechanisms.\nThe first is token footprint.\nByte-level BPE tokenizers attach the leading space to the next word, so a double space or a no-break space changes the token of every word in the prompt (69 and 148 changed positions on average), while curly quotes touch a handful of tokens.\nThe second mechanism operates even at a matched footprint.\nA trailing \\texttt{\\textbackslash n\\textbackslash n} changes the last tokens before the assistant turn, the position that most directly conditions the first answer token.\nCRLF changes every line boundary the model uses to parse the prompt's structure.\nTypographic quotes and dashes, by contrast, are common in web pretraining text, and models appear to treat them as near-synonyms of their ASCII forms.\nThe GEE results support this reading: controlling for edit size shrinks the punctuation--whitespace gap but does not close it.\n\n{\\bf Why is code text so volatile but code correctness not?}\nA 512-token program has many places where an equally valid choice can differ, such as a variable name, a loop form or a blank line.\nGreedy decoding commits to one branch, and a small change in the prompt can tip an early near-tie.\nMost of these alternatives are equally correct, so 31\\% of perturbed generations change text without changing test outcomes.\nThe same reasoning explains the larger code noise floor: re-running an identical prompt in a different batch position already changes 6.2\\% of code outputs, up to 21\\% for \\qwenthree-0.6B, but only 0.02\\% of labels.\nWithout this control, about a sixth of the code-change effect would be misattributed to formatting.\n\n\\para{Implications for practice.}\nFirst, whitespace normalization matters more than punctuation normalization. Converting \\texttt{\\textbackslash r\\textbackslash n} to \\texttt{\\textbackslash n}, replacing no-break spaces with spaces, collapsing repeated spaces and stripping trailing whitespace removes the most disruptive edits we tested.\nSecond, a 2--5 point accuracy difference between two prompt variants on a small model can come from formatting alone. Evaluations should report results over several formattings and compare against an identical-prompt re-run.\nThird, chat templates silently normalize some formatting (Llama trims trailing whitespace) and not other formatting (\\qwentwo keeps it), so the same raw input can be safe for one serving stack and fragile for another.\nFinally, because net accuracy barely moves, formatting is a source of variance in individual predictions rather than a systematic bias. This matters most for per-item consistency, such as when the same document is classified twice through different pipelines.\n\n\\para{Limitations.}\nWe test only open models up to 14.8B parameters, since no commercial API was available, and frontier models may behave differently.\nMost checkpoints are AWQ 4-bit, and quantization may itself add sensitivity. bf16 \\qwenthree-8B is the least sensitive model, but it also differs in family and was served by a separate vLLM server, which raises its control noise.\nWe use greedy decoding only, so we measure the deterministic component of the effect, not shifts in the sampling distribution.\nThe edit set is a design choice. Two whitespace edits (\\cond{double_space}, \\cond{nbsp}) touch every word. Without them, whitespace flips 4.5\\% of labels (mean of \\cond{trailing_space}, \\cond{crlf} and \\cond{trailing_newlines}), still about twice the punctuation rate.\nPunctuation edits can only change the few quotes and dashes present, and a quote-heavy corpus could show larger effects. \\cond{ellipsis_char} rarely applied.\nIn code prompts, punctuation edits touch only docstrings and comments, so they are smaller than in classification prompts.\nNear-constant predictions from Llama-3.2-1B on \\boolq and \\qwenthree-0.6B on \\agnews understate small-model sensitivity on those tasks.\nFinally, we use one prompt template per task, 400 items per classification task and 464 code problems, and other templates may be more or less fragile.\n\n\\para{Broader impact.}\nOur findings help practitioners build more reliable evaluations and pipelines. They could also inform adversarial manipulation of model outputs through invisible characters.\nBecause the effects we observe are mostly symmetric and concentrated on uncertain items, simple input normalization is an effective defence.\nEOF\ncat > sections/conclusion.tex <<'EOF'\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe study whether invisible, meaning-preserving formatting edits change LLM outputs.\nAcross 11 open models, five tasks and 239,616 greedy generations, a single invisible edit flips about 5\\% of classification labels (7.5\\% for models under 3B, 3.4\\% for larger ones), more than 200 times the rate of an identical re-run.\nContrary to our hypothesis, whitespace edits (no-break spaces, double spaces, CRLF, trailing spaces and newlines) are 2--5$\\times$ more disruptive than typographic punctuation, and token footprint explains only part of this gap.\nCode text changes in 36\\% of generations, but code correctness flips about as often as classification labels, and net accuracy barely moves.\nThe key takeaway is that \\emph{whitespace is not neutral}: normalize it before inference, and evaluate prompts under several formattings against an identical-prompt baseline.\n\nFuture work should test frontier API models and design matched-token-budget edits that separate \\emph{what} changes from \\emph{how many} tokens change.\nOther directions include logit-margin analysis to test whether flips occur only when the top two labels are close, measuring distribution shift under sampling rather than greedy flips, and studying whitespace inside code bodies for code-repair tasks.\nEOF\ncat > appendix/details.tex <<'EOF'\n\\section{Additional Experimental Details}\n\\label{app:details}\n\n\\para{Model checkpoints.}\n\\Tabref{tab:models} lists the exact checkpoints.\nAll local models run in vLLM 0.32 on shared RTX A6000 GPUs. \\qwenthree-8B is served by a separate vLLM instance through its OpenAI-compatible API.\nGeneration took about three hours in total, from 3--10 minutes for 0.5--1.5B models to about 1h40 for the 14B model.\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\small\n    \\begin{tabular}{@{}llcc@{}}\n        \\toprule\n        \\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Checkpoint} & \\textbf{Params (B)} & \\textbf{Precision} \\\\\n        \\midrule\n        \\qwentwo-\\{0.5,1.5,3,7,14\\}B & \\texttt{Qwen/Qwen2.5-\\{\\ldots\\}-Instruct-AWQ} & 0.5--14.8 & AWQ-INT4 \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-1B & \\texttt{unsloth/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct} & 1.2 & bf16 \\\\\n        Llama-3.2-3B & \\texttt{AMead10/Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct-AWQ} & 3.2 & AWQ \\\\\n        Llama-3.1-8B & \\texttt{hugging-quants/Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct-AWQ-INT4} & 8.0 & AWQ \\\\\n        \\qwenthree-0.6B & \\texttt{Qwen/Qwen3-0.6B} (thinking off) & 0.6 & bf16 \\\\\n        \\qwenthree-4B & \\texttt{Qwen/Qwen3-4B-AWQ} (thinking off) & 4.0 & AWQ \\\\\n        \\qwenthree-8B & \\texttt{Qwen/Qwen3-8B} via vLLM server (thinking off) & 8.2 & bf16 \\\\\n        \\bottomrule\n    \\end{tabular}\n    \\caption{Model checkpoints. Small models ($<$3B) are \\qwentwo-0.5B, \\qwentwo-1.5B, Llama-3.2-1B and \\qwenthree-0.6B.}\n    \\label{tab:models}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\para{Prompt templates.}\nThe classification instructions are:\n\\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=0pt,topsep=2pt]\n    \\item \\sst: \\texttt{Classify the sentiment of the movie review below as \"positive\" or \"negative\".} \\texttt{Don't explain - answer with one word only.} \\texttt{Review: \\ldots{} Sentiment:}\n    \\item \\agnews: the same format with the four topics \\texttt{\"World\", \"Sports\", \"Business\" or \"Sci/Tech\"}.\n    \\item \\boolq: \\texttt{Read the passage and answer the question with \"yes\" or \"no\".} followed by the same answer-format line, the passage and the question.\n\\end{itemize}\nFor \\humaneval, the prompt reads: \\texttt{Complete the following Python function. Don't change its signature - return the complete function \\ldots{} in a single \\`{}\\`{}\\`{}python code block, without \"explanations\" or tests.}, followed by the function stub.\nFor \\mbpp, the prompt contains the task text, \\texttt{Your code should pass these tests:} and the three asserts.\nThe double quotes, the apostrophe in ``Don't'' and the spaced hyphen ensure that every punctuation edit except \\cond{ellipsis_char} changes every prompt.\n\n\\para{Code-pass/fail flips by edit size.}\n\\Figref{fig:heat_code} shows the per-model pass/fail flip rate for code.\nAs with classification, whitespace columns dominate, especially \\cond{double_space} and \\cond{tabs}.\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n    \\centering\n    \\includegraphics[width=0.8\\linewidth]{figures/heat_code_flip.png}\n    \\caption{Code pass/fail flip rate (\\%) per model and edit, pooled over \\humaneval and \\mbpp. Whitespace edits produce the largest functional changes across models.}\n    \\label{fig:heat_code}\n\\end{figure}\nEOF","description":"Write discussion, conclusion, appendix"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01KZE2dTZLgiS3V44FRhboV6":{"cwd":"/data/summereunann/neurico-workspaces/invisible_formatting_effects_20261029_120001_qr78st90/paper_draft"}}}
