{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgXFYUA5mPJuoqw6dgxcB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The paper is written and compiles cleanly: `paper_draft/main.pdf` is 12 pages, with no LaTeX errors, undefined references or overfull boxes. The main text ends on page 9, followed by references and a one-section appendix.\n\n**Title:** *Invisible Formatting, Visible Effects: Whitespace Changes Flip LLM Outputs More Often Than Punctuation Changes* (author: NeuriCo).\n\n**What's in it**\n- **Abstract and introduction:** follow the style guide's order (hook, why it matters, gap, approach, numbers, then four contribution bullets). The headline result is that whitespace edits flip 7.1% of labels and punctuation edits 2.2%, the reverse of the hypothesis. Every edit is measured against the 0.02% rate from re-running the identical prompt.\n- **Related work:** four themes: prompt-format sensitivity, character-level robustness, tokenization, and nondeterminism during inference.\n- **Method:** a short formal definition of divergence, the edits and control in a table, tasks, models, metrics, statistics and the reproducibility checks.\n- **Results:** five tables (overall, per edit, per model, model size, task) and three figures (per-edit bars, classification heatmap, size trend), with 95% CIs, McNemar/Holm counts and the GEE odds ratios. A final subsection covers what drives the effect: whether the edit survives the chat template, edit size, which items flip, and accuracy.\n- **Discussion:** a verdict on each of the four hypotheses, the likely mechanism, practical advice, and the report's limitations.\n- **Appendix:** exact model checkpoints, prompt templates and the code pass/fail heatmap.\n\nAll numbers come from REPORT.md. I checked the figure-based claims against the figures themselves, and fixed one table cell that had mixed a code value into the classification column.\n\n**Things to check before using it:**\n- **\"Preprint.\" footer:** the NeurIPS style file fails to compile with `[final]` unless a track is chosen, and every track footer claims acceptance at NeurIPS 2025. I kept the required preamble line and added one line to `main.tex` that sets the footer to \"Preprint.\" instead.\n- **Unverified references:** there was no literature review, so the bibliography in `references.bib` was written from background knowledge and not checked against the original sources. This includes the citations I added beyond the report's list: typo robustness, TextFlint, BPE tokenization, the nondeterminism paper by Atil et al., AWQ, and the model technical reports. Several entries also shorten their author lists to \"and others\".\n- **Outline:** I also saved a short outline to `paper/OUTLINE.md`, as the skill asks.\n\nNothing has been committed."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1207,"cache_read_input_tokens":106893,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1207},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a7259184-e90b-461a-a0ed-e4b7ad9b7a2d","uuid":"fed7ec65-ba7a-4328-83cc-c4e55ec3013d","timestamp":"2026-10-29T21:38:35.449Z","request_id":"req_011CgXFYTyB34d7vMQdm3N2p"}
